# Source preparation pipeline to be run from mapterhorn/pipelines folder.
# Mosaic of all 4 N41/N42 x E140/E141 1-degree Copernicus GLO-30 tiles,
# cropped to lon 140.35-141.35, lat 41.55-42.15 -- generously covering
# jphakodatecity1/5m/10m's own real extent (lon 140.55-141.20, lat
# 41.70-42.00) on every side. Second expansion this session: the
# original 0.3x0.3deg crop (D11) missed ~12km of even the small
# Hakodate/Goryokaku *trial*'s southern extent; this crop is sized for
# the much larger real jphakodatecity1 land coverage instead, with the
# same generous-margin lesson applied up front rather than discovered
# the hard way again. See mapterhorn-japan-bridge's DECISIONS.md D11
# for the investigation that led here.
#
# This is a low-priority global fallback (maxzoom forced down to
# macrotile_z=12 by aggregation_covering.py, well below the 1m sources'
# 16, so it can never win their equal-maxzoom tie-break) so that open
# ocean renders as real (near-0m) elevation instead of nodata, and
# doubles as a universal ~30m fallback for any land gap the 1m/5m/10m
# sources don't cover. Mirrors how upstream Mapterhorn's own published
# data blends both in via its own global `glo30` source-catalog entry --
# confirmed by reading upstream's source-catalog directly (no separate
# bathymetry/ocean-specific source exists there either; GLO-30 alone is
# upstream's universal fallback, land and sea both). Filtered from the
# full source-catalog/glo30 (24,674 global tiles) rather than reusing
# that entry directly, so a bridge run never accidentally pulls the
# whole planet.
[no-cd]
default:
    uv run python source_download.py jphakodatetrialsea
    uv run python source_bounds.py jphakodatetrialsea
    uv run python source_polygonize.py jphakodatetrialsea 4
